{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Sachs Harbour Shallows: Sea Ice Diatoms and the Bacterial Understory",
    "date": "2026-08-31",
    "lesson": "Beneath thinning Arctic fast ice near Sachs Harbour, a pennate diatom bloom rides the underside of the ice, gliding on its raphe through brine channels toward the light. As diatoms exude dissolved carbon and eventually die, heterotrophic bacteria below them switch on to harvest that leaked and released carbon. This teaches vertical coupling between phototrophs and heterotrophs in a physically confined, porous ice habitat, and shows how a genuinely mobile diatom (pennate, raphe-gliding) differs from a static centric one. The scene is cold, slow and food-limited except right at the ice-water interface, where the bloom concentrates everything a heterotroph needs.",
    "citation": "Arctic sea-ice algal bloom dynamics and ice-bacteria coupling (e.g. Mundy et al., under-ice bloom studies, Beaufort Sea/Sachs Harbour region)",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "Under-ice pennate diatom blooms and brine-channel bacterial coupling, Beaufort Sea fast ice near Sachs Harbour, NWT"
  },
